# Overview

Within a gene, `spiDE` tests several correlated `celltype:condition:niche`
(*ResponseNiche*) coefficients and must collapse them into a single gene-level
p-value and one p-value per index cell type. The default combiner is **Brown's
method** — a correlation-aware generalisation of Fisher's method that consumes
the coefficient correlation matrix. This vignette evaluates an alternative, the
**Cauchy combination test** (ACAT), which *ignores* the correlation structure
entirely yet is reported in the literature to remain well-calibrated even under
strong positive dependence.

The combiner is selectable via the `combine` argument of `testSpiDE()` /
`spiDE()`:

```{r api, eval = FALSE}
res <- spiDE(spe, condition = "condition", combine = "cauchy") # default
res <- spiDE(spe, condition = "condition", combine = "brown")  # alternative
```

The results below motivated making **Cauchy the default combiner**; Brown is
retained as an option for back-compatibility.

The assessment has two tiers:

1. an **isolated Monte Carlo** that feeds both combiners identical, exactly
   correlated p-values so their behaviour is compared in a controlled setting;
2. a **full-pipeline** comparison on the packaged toy dataset, where only the
   combiner differs.

```{r libs, message = FALSE}
library(spiDE)
library(ggplot2)
theme_set(theme_bw(base_size = 11))
methods_pal <- c(Brown = "#1b7837", Cauchy = "#762a83")
```

# The two combiners

Given $m$ one-sided p-values $p_1,\dots,p_m$ for a gene:

* **Brown's method** treats $-2\sum\log p_j$ as a scaled $\chi^2$ whose
  scale/df are adjusted for the correlation $R$ of the underlying statistics.
  `spiDE` estimates $R$ from the Wald coefficient covariance and combines with
  `poolr::fisher(adjust = "generalized")`. It is *exact* under the assumed
  multivariate-normal dependence but needs a well-conditioned $R$.
* **Cauchy combination (ACAT)** forms
  $T = \tfrac{1}{m}\sum_j \tan\{(0.5 - p_j)\pi\}$ and returns
  $1 - F_{\text{Cauchy}}(T)$. Because a weighted sum of Cauchy variates is
  Cauchy regardless of dependence, the tail is (asymptotically) valid under
  *arbitrary* correlation, with no $R$ to estimate.

The functions below reproduce exactly what the package does internally
(`.cauchyCombine()` and the `poolr` call in `.waldBrownGene()`).

```{r combiners}
# exchangeable-correlated standard normals via a shared common factor:
#   Z_j = sqrt(rho) * U + sqrt(1 - rho) * E_j   =>   cor(Z_i, Z_j) = rho
rmvnorm_exch <- function(n, k, rho, mean = 0) {
  u <- rnorm(n)
  e <- matrix(rnorm(n * k), n, k)
  sqrt(rho) * u + sqrt(1 - rho) * e + mean
}

cauchy_combine <- function(P) {                    # ACAT, matches .cauchyCombine
  P <- pmin(pmax(P, 1e-15), 1 - 1e-15)
  1 - pcauchy(rowMeans(tan((0.5 - P) * pi)))
}

brown_combine <- function(P, R) {                  # Brown, matches .waldBrownGene
  Rm <- poolr::mvnconv(R, target = "m2lp", side = 1, cov2cor = FALSE)
  apply(P, 1, function(p) {
    poolr::fisher(p, side = 1, R = Rm, adjust = "generalized")$p
  })
}
```

# Tier 1: isolated Monte Carlo

We draw $m$ correlated z-scores with exchangeable correlation $\rho$, turn them
into one-sided p-values $p_j = \Pr(Z > z_j)$, and combine. Under the null the
z-scores have mean 0 and the combined p-value should be Uniform(0, 1).

```{r sim-grid}
nsim  <- 3000
rhos  <- c(0, 0.3, 0.6, 0.9)
ks    <- c(3, 5, 10)

simulate_null <- function(k, rho) {
  R <- matrix(rho, k, k); diag(R) <- 1
  Z <- rmvnorm_exch(nsim, k, rho)
  P <- pnorm(Z, lower.tail = FALSE)
  data.frame(
    Brown  = brown_combine(P, R),
    Cauchy = cauchy_combine(P)
  )
}

null_res <- do.call(rbind, lapply(ks, function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(rhos, function(rho) {
    d <- simulate_null(k, rho)
    data.frame(k = k, rho = rho,
               method = rep(c("Brown", "Cauchy"), each = nsim),
               p = c(d$Brown, d$Cauchy))
  }))
}))
```

## Null calibration (uniformity)

A perfectly calibrated test lies on the diagonal of a p-value QQ plot. We show
$m = 5$ across the four correlation levels.

```{r qq, fig.height = 4.6}
qq_df <- do.call(rbind, by(
  subset(null_res, k == 5), list(null_res$method[null_res$k == 5],
                                 null_res$rho[null_res$k == 5]),
  function(d) {
    d <- d[order(d$p), ]
    d$expected <- (seq_len(nrow(d)) - 0.5) / nrow(d)
    d
  }
))

ggplot(qq_df, aes(-log10(expected), -log10(p), colour = method)) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2, colour = "grey50") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~ paste0("rho = ", rho), nrow = 1) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = methods_pal) +
  labs(x = expression(-log[10]("expected")),
       y = expression(-log[10]("observed")),
       colour = NULL, title = "Null QQ plots (m = 5 correlated p-values)") +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

At $\rho = 0$ both track the diagonal. As correlation grows, **Brown** (which is
handed the true $R$) stays on the diagonal, while **Cauchy** bends slightly
below it in the body — mildly conservative in the bulk — but the two converge
again in the extreme tail that actually drives FDR.

## Type-I error at $\alpha = 0.05$

```{r typeI}
typeI <- aggregate(p ~ method + k + rho, null_res,
                   function(x) mean(x <= 0.05))
names(typeI)[names(typeI) == "p"] <- "typeI"

ggplot(typeI, aes(rho, typeI, colour = method)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = 2, colour = "grey50") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  facet_wrap(~ paste0("m = ", k), nrow = 1) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = methods_pal) +
  ylim(0, 0.1) +
  labs(x = expression(rho), y = "empirical type-I error (nominal 0.05)",
       colour = NULL, title = "Type-I error vs correlation") +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

```{r typeI-table}
knitr::kable(
  reshape(typeI, idvar = c("k", "rho"), timevar = "method",
          direction = "wide"),
  digits = 3, row.names = FALSE,
  col.names = c("m", "rho", "Brown", "Cauchy"),
  caption = "Empirical type-I error at alpha = 0.05."
)
```

Both combiners control type-I error across every correlation level; neither is
anti-conservative. Cauchy is marginally conservative as $\rho \to 0.9$,
consistent with the QQ plots.

## Power under the alternative

We now shift a subset of the $m$ columns by a common effect $\delta$ (the rest
stay null) and measure the detection rate at the 0.05 threshold, sweeping
$\delta$ at a challenging $\rho = 0.6$, $m = 5$ with 2 signal columns.

```{r power}
delta_grid <- seq(0, 3, by = 0.5)
k <- 5; rho <- 0.6; n_signal <- 2

power_res <- do.call(rbind, lapply(delta_grid, function(delta) {
  R <- matrix(rho, k, k); diag(R) <- 1
  mu <- c(rep(delta, n_signal), rep(0, k - n_signal))
  Z <- rmvnorm_exch(nsim, k, rho) + matrix(mu, nsim, k, byrow = TRUE)
  P <- pnorm(Z, lower.tail = FALSE)
  data.frame(
    delta = delta,
    method = c("Brown", "Cauchy"),
    power = c(mean(brown_combine(P, R) <= 0.05),
              mean(cauchy_combine(P) <= 0.05))
  )
}))

ggplot(power_res, aes(delta, power, colour = method)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = methods_pal) +
  labs(x = expression(paste("effect size ", delta,
                            " (2 of 5 columns)")),
       y = "power at alpha = 0.05", colour = NULL,
       title = "Power vs effect size (rho = 0.6)") +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

The two curves are near-identical: Cauchy sacrifices essentially no power to
gain its distribution-free calibration, and is marginally ahead once the signal
is strong (its heavy tail rewards a single very small p-value).

# Tier 2: the full spiDE pipeline

We now run the entire pipeline on the packaged toy dataset — which carries a
planted signal (gene `G1` up-regulated in index cell type `A`, driven by the
local density of niche cell type `B`) — under both combiners, changing nothing
else.

```{r pipeline, message = FALSE}
data(toySpiDE)
spe <- buildNiches(toySpiDE, sigma = c(10, 30, 50))

fit_brown  <- testSpiDE(fitSpiDE(spe, condition = "condition", verbose = FALSE),
                        spe = spe, fdr = 1, combine = "brown")
fit_cauchy <- testSpiDE(fitSpiDE(spe, condition = "condition", verbose = FALSE),
                        spe = spe, fdr = 1, combine = "cauchy")

tb <- results(fit_brown)
tc <- results(fit_cauchy)
```

## Recovery of the planted G1 / A / B signal

```{r recovery}
planted <- function(tab, method) {
  x <- tab[tab$gene == "G1" & tab$ct_index == "A" & tab$ct_niche == "B", ]
  g1a <- tab[tab$gene == "G1" & tab$ct_index == "A", ]
  data.frame(
    method = method,
    direction = x$DirectionNiche,
    t = round(x$t, 2),
    fdr.niche = signif(x$fdr.niche, 3),
    strongest_niche_for_G1A = g1a$ct_niche[which.max(abs(g1a$t))]
  )
}
knitr::kable(rbind(planted(tb, "Brown"), planted(tc, "Cauchy")),
             row.names = FALSE,
             caption = "Both combiners recover the planted G1/A/B effect.")
```

Both flag the same effect, in the same (Up) direction, with `B` as the strongest
niche for `G1` in index `A` — the combiner does not disturb signal recovery.

## Agreement across all genes

```{r agreement, fig.height = 4.2}
m <- merge(
  tb[, c("gene", "ct_index", "ct_niche", "fdr.niche")],
  tc[, c("gene", "ct_index", "ct_niche", "fdr.niche")],
  by = c("gene", "ct_index", "ct_niche"), suffixes = c(".brown", ".cauchy")
)

ggplot(m, aes(-log10(fdr.niche.brown), -log10(fdr.niche.cauchy))) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2, colour = "grey50") +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.5, colour = "#2166ac") +
  labs(x = expression(-log[10]("niche FDR (Brown)")),
       y = expression(-log[10]("niche FDR (Cauchy)")),
       title = "Per-(gene, index, niche) FDRs agree closely")
```

```{r agreement-cor}
cor_val <- cor(-log10(m$fdr.niche.brown), -log10(m$fdr.niche.cauchy),
               method = "spearman")
```

The two rankings are highly concordant (Spearman
$\rho = `r round(cor_val, 3)`$ on the niche-level FDRs).

# Summary

```{r verdict, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(data.frame(
  Property = c("Correlation handling", "Needs correlation matrix",
               "Type-I control (all rho)", "Power", "Dependency"),
  Brown = c("explicit (uses R)", "yes", "nominal", "reference", "poolr"),
  Cauchy = c("distribution-free", "no", "nominal, mildly conservative at high rho",
             "matches Brown", "none")
), caption = "Brown vs Cauchy at a glance.")
```

**Verdict.** On both the controlled Monte Carlo and the full pipeline, Cauchy
combination matches or beats Brown's method: it controls type-I error at every
correlation level, loses no meaningful power, recovers the planted signal
identically, and does so without estimating a correlation matrix or depending on
`poolr`. Its only cost is mild conservativeness in the p-value *body* under very
strong dependence — irrelevant for the extreme-tail regime that FDR control
depends on.

**These results motivate making Cauchy combination the default** for the
within-gene combination step (`combine = "cauchy"`). Brown's method is retained
as a selectable alternative (`combine = "brown"`) for back-compatibility and for
settings where an explicit, correlation-aware combiner is preferred.

